\chapter{Rewriting and optimization contracts}
An optimizer must preserve the behavior that the language actually defines.
For pure arithmetic, replacing an addition by zero can be straightforward.
For effectful expressions, removing a seemingly redundant operand may remove
a library call or change which error occurs first.

\section{Separate pure and effectful nodes}
Use AST tags or a schema to distinguish literal computation from calls into
the host. A rewriting pass can then operate on pure subtrees while preserving
evaluation order at effectful boundaries. The rule should record its
preconditions rather than infer them from a convenient example.

MuDSL offers direct rewrite sets, first-order term rewriting, and equality
saturation. Direct rewriting selects a particular replacement. E-graphs retain
equivalent forms and extract a result according to a cost. Term graphs preserve
sharing when reduction changes a common subexpression. Each approach has a
different identity and termination contract.

\section{Bound the search}
Cycles in rewrite rules are easy to write. Use iteration, node, or step limits
and report whether a fixpoint was reached. An interrupted optimizer can still
return a useful result if the API distinguishes completion from exhaustion.

MuLL currently evaluates its syntax directly. Its left-to-right effects and
short-circuit operators are part of the language contract. Any future compiled
backend must preserve those rules, including the fact that a failed library
load updates the error text used by the fallback expression.
